WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:04.633
- So Livermorium was first made in around 2000.

00:00:04.667 --> 00:00:10.933
It was made in calcium-48 plus curium-248 reaction.

00:00:10.967 --> 00:00:14.600
We've since made 4 different isotopes of Livermorium.

00:00:14.633 --> 00:00:18.933
They all have tens of milliseconds half-lives, so they're pretty short-lived.

00:00:18.967 --> 00:00:21.100
So unfortunately I can't show you a bottle.

00:00:21.133 --> 00:00:28.300
It was a very interesting process to name these
elements when we got permission to name

00:00:28.333 --> 00:00:32.067
114 and 116-were naming two elements at once.

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:38.233
We of course began discussions with our Russian colleagues and reached a concensus

00:00:38.267 --> 00:00:42.567
on these names, Flerovium for 114 and Livermorium for 116.

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:49.033
We chose Livermorium because we
could not discover enough elements

00:00:49.067 --> 00:00:54.300
to name one after everybody who was
important in these kinds of experiments.

00:00:54.333 --> 00:00:57.333
There was many, many scientists here at Livermore.

00:00:57.367 --> 00:01:02.933
Some have retired, even passed away, who
were involved in heavy element research.

00:01:02.967 --> 00:01:08.167
So we couldn't discover enough elements
and name after every single person.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:15.267
So we chose Livermorium to honor all those people,
honor the institution here and the city of Livermore.

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:21.600
We thought this was an appropriate way to kind of
recognize all of those scientists involved in that work.

00:01:21.633 --> 00:01:26.533
- You spent some time in the community.
I know you talk with scholars, you know people.

00:01:26.567 --> 00:01:30.700
How has that decision been received by the institution and the people of Livermore?

00:01:30.733 --> 00:01:34.367
They must...
- It's been outstanding.

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:40.367
The mayor of Livermore is a water chemist,
so he understands chemistry.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:44.267
He was ecstatic that this came about.

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:51.633
He understood the significance of a new element
and that particular name on the periodic table.

00:01:51.667 --> 00:01:57.600
He's been very supportive. We have a Livermorium day now, which is in late May.

00:01:57.633 --> 00:02:06.767
They've also named a part of a park, a Plaza in downtown Livermore: Livermorium Plaza.

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:11.133
They've been supportive, very
ecstatic about it, and understand...

00:02:11.167 --> 00:02:20.567
I think this community here, because this lab is here, people are a little more scientifically understanding

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:25.233
or scientifically literate, So they understand the significance of it.

00:02:25.267 --> 00:02:28.433
So it's been nothing but supportive for that element.

00:02:28.467 --> 00:02:32.967
So one of the predicted properties,
because it's in group 16,

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:41.733
It's in the column in the periodic table where
many of the hydrogen compounds of that group

00:02:41.767 --> 00:02:47.267
are very smelly. So think about hydrogen sulfide - the rotten eggs smell.

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:50.800
Hydrogen selenide.
- Oh, Selenium...

00:02:50.833 --> 00:02:57.167
- Yes. Selenium is notorious for being very smelly.
Rotten garlic or something like that,

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:01.800
It's described at. Telluride. The telluride, tellurium, is also very smelly.

00:03:01.833 --> 00:03:08.367
So if you think about going down
that column in the periodic table,

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:14.000
you might say that Livermorium is maybe the smelliest element on the periodic table.

00:03:14.033 --> 00:03:22.833
It would be very difficult to verify, because we haven't made the hydrogen compound of it.

00:03:22.867 --> 00:03:27.933
The isotopes, as I mentioned, are 10
milliseconds long, so it's very difficult.

00:03:27.967 --> 00:03:32.433
And, in addition, you probably wouldn't want to smell it, because it's radioactive

00:03:32.467 --> 00:03:35.267
and you wouldn't want to actually put your nose on it and smell it.

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:41.233
Hydrogen sulfide won't hurt you... it'll
hurt in strong-enough concentrations,

00:03:41.267 --> 00:03:46.933
but everyone smelled rotten eggs before.

00:03:46.967 --> 00:03:49.133
You wouldn't want to do that with Livermorium.

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:52.440
(Audio from "More from Superheavy Videos") So, let's keep going and see where the experiment is

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:57.380
So, as we go along here, the ions are going faster and faster and faster...

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:00.260
...all the time inside the tube

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:03.300
here you see such a target wheel

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:07.480
(...) which is mounted on the axis of this motor here

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:10.880
its ends like this

